    "recommendations": "Reducing direct brush contact with the structure and increasing separation between heavy brush concentrations and the building perimeter would meaningfully improve defensible space and reduce fuel continuity near the improvements. Vegetation and brush growing immediately adjacent to the structure should be managed to create a clear zone, as this direct contact represents the most actionable area within the property owner's control. Where scattered shrubs and mixed vegetation create connected fuel pathways across the property, selectively reducing continuity between plant groupings and the structure could further limit fire spread opportunities. The single access route and long driveway warrant particular attention, as maintaining adequate width, overhead clearance, and vegetation setbacks along this corridor would support firefighter access and may improve evacuation conditions. Keeping the driveway passable and ensuring turnaround or pullout areas remain unobstructed would support suppression operations. Ongoing management of brush and vegetation within the immediate structure perimeter, combined with sustained attention to the access route, could improve overall wildfire resilience for this property."
